---

<GuidePage
  title="Full Project Planner Tutorial"
  description="The field-by-field and result-by-result manual for turning an external quilt cut list and measured fabric into one shopping and cutting plan."
  eyebrow="Start Here · Step 2 of 2"
  guideSlug="project-planner-tutorial"
  guideCategory="start_here"
  previous={{ href: '/guides/getting-started/', label: 'Quick Start' }}
  next={{ href: '/guides/enter-a-cut-list/', label: 'Enter a Cut List' }}
>
  <nav class="guide-toc card" aria-labelledby="tutorial-contents">
    <h2 id="tutorial-contents">On this page</h2>
    <ol>
      {
        contents.map(([id, label]) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <section id="before-you-begin">
    <h2>1. Before you begin</h2><p>
      Gather the rectangular cut requirements for each project fabric. Measure
      any physical pieces you want to use. QuiltClarity does not need the quilt
      design itself and does not interpret pattern prose, PDFs, or images.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="project-and-units">
    <h2>2. Project and units</h2><h3>Project name</h3><p>
      <strong>What it means:</strong> an optional local label for the plan. <strong
        >What to enter:</strong
      > any name useful in print. It never enters analytics.
    </p><h3>Measurements</h3><p>
      Choose Inches and yards or Centimetres and metres before entering values.
      The calculation converts to exact internal millimetres; switching units
      converts existing numeric controls.
    </p><h3>Seam allowance</h3><p>
      The default is ¼ inch. It is added on every edge only when a requirement
      uses Finished size.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="fabrics">
    <h2>3. Fabrics</h2><p>
      Add each distinct fabric once and give it a practical name such as
      Background or Blue print. Every cut and stock piece retains this fabric
      identity; pieces never move between fabrics.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="usable-wof">
    <h2>4. Nominal vs usable WOF</h2><p>
      <strong>Nominal width</strong> is the descriptive bolt width before selvages.
      <strong>Usable WOF</strong> is the crosswise width actually available for cuts
      after the selvages you plan to remove. Packing uses usable WOF. The imperial
      default is 40 inches, but your measured or pattern-specific value is authoritative.
    </p><p>
      <strong>Common mistake:</strong> entering 42 inches because the bolt says 42
      even though only 40 inches will be cut.
    </p><p><a href="/guides/width-of-fabric/">Read the WOF reference</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="directional-fabric">
    <h2>5. Directional fabric and rotation</h2><p>
      Turn on Directional fabric when a motif or grain direction must remain
      aligned. Rotation swaps a piece's width and height only when the fabric
      default, piece override, orientation, and WOF-strip rules all allow it. A
      piece-level “Do not allow” setting wins.
    </p><p>
      <a href="/guides/directional-fabric-cutting/"
        >Read the directional-fabric reference</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="purchase-safety">
    <h2>6. Purchase safety and rounding</h2><p>
      Safety adds a percentage to newly purchased raw bolt length. The default
      is 5%. Purchase increment then rounds that buffered amount upward—never
      nearest or down. Safety does not enlarge stock you already own, and a
      stock-covered project remains a zero purchase.
    </p><p>
      <strong>Example:</strong> 10 inches raw with 5% safety becomes 10.5 inches,
      then rounds upward to the selected shop increment.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="fabric-you-have">
    <h2>7. Fabric you already have</h2><p>
      Enter only physical fabric pieces available to this project. Width is
      across the fabric; length is lengthwise. Each quantity expands into
      separate identifiable bins. QuiltClarity proves fit geometrically instead
      of subtracting areas or yardage totals.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="stock-presets">
    <h2>8. Stock presets and custom dimensions</h2><p>
      Fat quarter, fat eighth, quarter-yard, half-yard, partial-yardage, and
      custom actions fill convenient starting values. Dimensions remain visible
      and editable. Measure the actual piece: an edited 18 × 20-inch fat quarter
      is planned as 18 × 20, not 18 × 21.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="cut-requirements">
    <h2>9. Cut requirements</h2><p>
      Each row describes one repeated rectangular cut group and assigns it to a
      fabric. Add, duplicate, delete/undo, or paste rows. Calculation does not
      depend on visual row order.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="piece-label">
    <h2>10. Piece label</h2><p>
      Use a short label you will recognize at the cutting table, such as Blue
      corner square. It appears in instructions, diagrams, and text alternatives
      and stays local to your browser.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="quantity">
    <h2>11. Quantity</h2><p>
      Enter the number of identical pieces required. Quantity must be a positive
      whole number. If a construction method yields batches, calculate those
      unit cuts in the relevant calculator first.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="width-height">
    <h2>12. Width and height</h2><p>
      Enter the rectangle's two dimensions in the selected measurement system.
      Width is the initial across-fabric extent; height is the initial
      lengthwise extent. Allowed rotation may swap them.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="cut-vs-finished">
    <h2>13. Cut vs finished dimensions</h2><p>
      <strong>Cut</strong> uses the entered dimensions exactly. <strong
        >Finished</strong
      > describes the sewn size, so QuiltClarity adds twice the project seam allowance
      to both dimensions and shows the resolved cut size under More options.
    </p><p>
      <strong>Common mistake:</strong> choosing Finished for dimensions a pattern
      already labels as cut.
    </p><p>
      <a href="/guides/finished-vs-cut-size/"
        >Read the finished-vs-cut reference</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="wof-strips">
    <h2>14. WOF strips and orientation constraints</h2><p>
      A width-of-fabric strip spans the full usable crosswise width and consumes
      its strip width down the bolt. It cannot rotate into a lengthwise strip or
      fit a stock bin narrower than usable WOF. Crosswise and lengthwise
      orientation constraints likewise preserve the required fabric axis.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="paste-cut-list">
    <h2>15. Spreadsheet paste</h2><p>
      Use <strong>Paste from spreadsheet</strong> for CSV or tab-separated data in
      the documented columns: Fabric, Label, Qty, Width, Height, Size mode. Review
      the preview, fix malformed cells, map unmatched fabrics explicitly, then confirm.
      QuiltClarity never guesses cut requirements from prose.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="pattern-yardage">
    <h2>16. Pattern yardage comparison</h2><p>
      Pattern says and Pattern usable WOF are optional per fabric. QuiltClarity
      compares the stated amount with a separate all-new-fabric scenario using
      the cuts you entered. A WOF mismatch produces a not-like-for-like warning.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="calculate">
    <h2>17. Calculate and recalculate</h2><p>
      Select Calculate Cutting Plan after editing. Complex planning does not run
      on every keystroke. If an input changes after a result, the old result
      remains visible but is marked <strong>Plan needs recalculation</strong>;
      select Recalculate before acting on it.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="project-status">
    <h2>18. Project status</h2><p>
      The first result states whether every cut is covered by stock, additional
      purchase is needed, or requirements remain uncovered. Blocking input
      errors prevent a result; warnings preserve a usable result and explain the
      limitation.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="shopping-list">
    <h2>19. Shopping list</h2><p>
      This is the actionable per-fabric purchase summary. It separates raw
      additional plan, the amount with safety, and the shop-rounded Buy now
      amount.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="buy-now">
    <h2>20. Buy now</h2><p>
      <strong>What it means:</strong> the purchase remaining after exact stock allocation.
      <strong>What it includes:</strong> safety and upward purchase rounding on new
      fabric. <strong>What it does not mean:</strong> the pattern amount or all-new-fabric
      requirement.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="fresh-fabric-plan">
    <h2>21. Fresh-fabric plan</h2><p>
      This hypothetical scenario plans all entered cuts from a new bolt under
      current assumptions and ignores stock. It provides the fair basis for
      pattern comparison, so it can be nonzero when Buy now is zero.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="pattern-says">
    <h2>22. Pattern says</h2><p>
      This is the external amount you entered. It may include construction
      method, trimming, shrinkage, squaring, miscuts, fussy cutting, direction,
      or designer preference. A difference is information—not proof of a pattern
      error.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="stock-allocation">
    <h2>23. Existing-stock allocations</h2><p>
      Each physical stock bin shows the exact pieces placed into it, cutting
      instructions, geometry, and a text equivalent. Bin labels remain distinct.
      An allocation of two remnants is never presented as one joined rectangle.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="purchased-layout">
    <h2>24. Purchased-fabric layout</h2><p>
      If stock leaves a shortfall, this section shows the exact remaining pieces
      on a fixed-width, variable-length purchased bolt. Raw length and rounded
      purchase are separate so you can audit the shop quantity.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="cutting-plan">
    <h2>25. Cutting instructions and diagram</h2><p>
      Instructions and SVG are projected from the same selected placements.
      Follow named stock bins first, then the purchased-bolt plan. When geometry
      cannot be truthfully simplified into strips, use the exact diagram and
      piece list. The layout is practical and deterministic, not a proven global
      optimum.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="leftovers">
    <h2>26. Leftovers</h2><p>
      Useful remaining regions are deterministic rectangles from the selected
      stock geometry. Separate rectangles are listed separately; QuiltClarity
      does not promise they combine into one contiguous remnant.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="warnings">
    <h2>27. Assumptions and warnings</h2><p>
      Check usable WOF, seam allowance, safety, purchase increment, rotation,
      direction, and method disclosures before buying or cutting.
      Correctness-critical caveats stay visible even when a ? control offers
      more explanation.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="print-plan">
    <h2>28. Print</h2><p>
      Print includes project identity/date, shopping, comparisons, allocations,
      instructions, assumptions, warnings, diagrams, and text alternatives.
      Editing controls and navigation are omitted. Diagram pages choose portrait
      or landscape and remain grayscale-safe.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="common-errors">
    <h2>29. Common errors</h2><ul>
      <li>Using nominal width instead of usable WOF.</li><li>
        Entering cut dimensions as Finished.
      </li><li>Combining separate remnants into one rectangle.</li><li>
        Allowing rotation for a directional piece.
      </li><li>Reading Buy now as the fresh-fabric or pattern amount.</li><li>
        Acting on a result marked Plan needs recalculation.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section id="special-cases">
    <h2>30. Common special cases</h2><p>
      For a cut list copied from a spreadsheet, use the deterministic paste
      preview. For WOF strips or directional fabric, open More options. For one
      stock rectangle and one repeated piece, use <a
        href="/calculators/pieces-from-fabric/">Pieces from Fabric</a
      >. For backing, batting, binding, triangle units, blocks, borders, or
      sashing, use the focused calculators.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuidePage>
